// TEST: Pipeline Three Stage Decline (rejects)
// REJECT-AT: codegen
// ERROR-CONTAINS: staged emission supports two stages
// WARN-CODEGEN: staged emission supports two stages
// (No `// ERROR: BL7004` pin: the harness emits and compiles directly rather
// than through the driver, and BL7004 is minted by the driver's
// takeCodegenRefusalDiagnostics drain -- so on this path the `#error` TEXT is
// the only checkable pin. Same reason fallback/009 and 178 name the code in
// prose only.)
// ============================================================================
// The INTERSECTION of 176 and 179, which neither covers on its own.
//
// 176 is a three-stage `>>@` chain that FUSES: every stage kernel is an
// expression, so the chain collapses into one loop and staged emission is
// never asked for. 179 is a two-stage chain that DECLINES (a block-bodied
// named function has no expression to inline) and falls back to the two-stage
// staged emitter, which handles it.
//
// Put both properties in one program -- three stages, one of them block-bodied
// -- and the chain reaches `genComposeApply` with a whole COMPOSITION sitting
// in a stage slot. That slot holds a kernel; a composition is neither a named
// C++ lambda nor a resolvable callable, so the per-stage fallback emitted
// `r__s1[__i0] = ((void)0);` and g++ delivered "void value not ignored as it
// ought to be" -- no BL code, no Blade line, nothing naming the limitation.
//
// Pinned here as the refusal it should always have been: a v1 ceiling with a
// sentence saying what to write instead.
// ============================================================================
let A = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
function blocky(x: Float) -> Float = { x * 2.0 }
let f = lambda(x) -> x + 1.0
let g = lambda(x) -> x - 1.0
let r = (object_for(f) >>@ object_for(blocky) >>@ object_for(g)) <@> A |> compute
